<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thessaloniki</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salonica, Salonika, Thessalonica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thessaloniki is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the main port of Macedonia, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a>. Founded around 315 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, it was a leading city of the Roman and Byzantine empires and, under Ottoman rule from 1430, home to a large Sephardic Jewish community. It became Greek in 1912, and most of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thessaloniki.jpg" alt="Thessaloniki" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tessaloniki_BW_2017-10-05_18-22-47.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
